  "notes": "The fullest adjacency description in the book and the only design with a tower: 'the tower is a prominent feature, but, as will be perceived, it is not a mere adjunct, but forms an intimate portion of the mansion itself.' Every door below is stated in the prose, which is why the adjacency confidence is high and the dimension confidence is nothing - not one room is measured. Two things worth having: the lobby under the stairs exists so servants can get from the kitchen to the dining room without crossing the hall, and the proprietor's suite is shut off from the rest of the first floor by a single door - 'A desirable privacy is thus obtained, and the windows of these rooms command views on three sides of the house.' Two servants' bedrooms may be added by carrying up the storey at the rear. The tower room is reached by a narrow stair cut out of the bedroom below it, and the lantern over the curved roof is there to ventilate the smoking room. 3000 pounds.",
